{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"mythopoeic-swirly-leafbird","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\n\nPlan review certified epic `1386-5e7c-391d-4a74` and two of its children with zero blocking\nfindings even though acceptance criteria across them were mutually unsatisfiable. Every\ncontradiction surfaced later — mid-implementation or at the completion-verifier close gate —\nand each correction forced a full LLM re-review because amending a description invalidates the\nattestation as `stale-material`. Bug `creamy-cocksure-elkhound` records the evidence (seven\ninstances in four failure classes). Until this lands, every plan author and every agent claiming\na ticket keeps paying that late-detection cost.\n\n## Approach\n\nTwo independent defects with two separate causes, fixed together because they share a test\nsurface.\n\n**(1) A new 1-TURN criterion `ac-satisfiability` (facet `coherence`).** It owns a concern no\ncriterion owns today: whether a ticket's own acceptance criteria and its Scope / Out-of-Scope\ndeclarations can all hold at once. `COH` covers contradictions BETWEEN sections and explicitly\ndisclaims within-section ones (\"not within-section nitpicks (those belong to E1/E2)\"); `E1`\ncovers criterion-to-description mapping, terminology and duplicates; `F1` covers measurability.\nNone asks whether the commitments are jointly satisfiable.\n\nThe rubric is written on a generic primitive rather than the motivating epic's subject matter:\n*an acceptance criterion quantifies over a set — universally (\"no file under X matches P\") or by\ncardinality (\"the N items are migrated\") — and something else in the same description places a\nmember inside the excepted region.* It names three shapes of that primitive:\nuniversal-vs-carve-out (a Scope or Out-of-Scope clause exempts a member of the set an AC\nquantifies over); derived-artifact closure (an AC asserts a property of an artifact the plan\nsays is regenerated from a source that keeps carrying the negation); and snapshot cardinality\n(an AC pins a count measured at authoring time for a set the plan says is discovered or\nrecomputed at execution time).\n\nPass-2 already grades exactly this defect: `internal_conflict` is a live verifier axis\n(`src/rebar/llm/review_kernel/verify_models.py:255`) and a member of `_PLAN_SEVERITY_AXES`\n(`src/rebar/llm/review_kernel/decide.py:309`). So the gap is confined to Pass-1 finding, and no\nverifier or impact-model change is part of this work. The criterion ships `advisory` at the\n`0.95` default; choosing a blocking posture needs replay data this change does not have yet.\n\nRejected: widening `COH` instead. It needs no new routing entry, but it puts two concerns in one\nsingle-turn scan and erases the boundary the rubrics police out loud. A separate routed\ncriterion is also close to free: `registry.chunk_by_facet`\n(`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/registry.py:434`) facet-sorts the single-turn tier then slices it\ninto fixed-size chunks, so chunk count is `ceil(total / n)` and one added criterion changes the\ncall count only when the applicable population is already an exact multiple of `n`. Single-turn\nchunks are never shed by the per-plan budget cap, so it cannot be starved on a large ticket the\nway an AGENT-tier alternative could.\n\n**(2) Make `G3`'s child-coverage audit able to fire.** `G3` asks whether the union of children\ncovers each parent acceptance criterion, and `plan_review_container.md` instructs it to flag an\nabsence \"only if NO sibling in the roster covers it\". But the roster it is handed carries ids\nand titles only (`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/pass1.py:154`), which cannot discharge that burden,\nso the anti-false-positive rule correctly silences the finding every time. Build the roster with\neach child's acceptance-criteria items — extracted with `evaluate_plan_clarity(...).ac_items`\n(`src/rebar/_plan_clarity.py:96`), the same helper the DET floor uses — and say so in `G3`'s\nrubric.\n\nThe roster is byte-identical across every pairing of one review, but it currently sits in\n`instructions` AFTER the per-pairing `children_block`, so it cannot be prefix-cached. Fattening\nit in place would re-send the epic's whole acceptance-criteria corpus once per pairing — cost\nquadratic in child count. Move it into the system prompt beside the parent plan, which\n`_resolve_system` (`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/passes.py:276`) keeps byte-stable inside the\ncached prefix, so the roster is written once and read thereafter. In `passes.py` this is a move,\nnot an addition: the roster line leaves `instructions` as it joins the system prompt, keeping\nthat file's length flat against the 800-LOC cap it sits near.\n\nRejected for (2): making the new criterion agentic so it fetches siblings itself. Cross-child\ncontradiction is already `G4`'s declared concern, so that would duplicate an owned concern and\nforce AGENT cost onto the cheap check. `G3`'s posture and threshold are deliberately left\nuntouched — revisiting them needs dogfooding data from this change.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/plan_review_ac_satisfiability.md` — new: the criterion rubric\n  (prompt id `plan-review-ac-satisfiability`, per the stem-underscores-to-dashes convention in\n  `prompts._packaged_prompt_files`).\n- `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/plan_review_G3.md` — rubric: discharge the sibling test against the\n  roster's acceptance criteria.\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/criteria_routing.json` — the `ac-satisfiability` routing entry.\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/registry.py` — add `ac-satisfiability` to `CANONICAL_LLM`.\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/pass1.py` — build the acceptance-criteria-bearing sibling roster.\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/passes.py` — move the roster into the cached system prompt.\n- `docs/plan-review-criteria-guide.md` — regenerated from the registry.\n- `tests/unit/test_ac_satisfiability_criterion.py` — new.\n- `tests/unit/test_container_roster.py` — new.\n\n## Testing\n\n- Happy path: with `ac-satisfiability` registered, `validate_packaged_routing()` returns an\n  empty list and `chunk_by_facet` places it in the facet-sorted `coherence` group alongside\n  `COH` and `E1`.\n- Happy path: the roster built for a parent whose children carry `## Acceptance Criteria`\n  sections contains each child's criterion text.\n- Edge: a child whose description has no `## Acceptance Criteria` section yields a roster entry\n  without raising.\n- Empty: a parent with no children yields an empty roster without raising.\n- Contract: the container pass carries the roster in its system prompt and not in its\n  per-pairing instructions — the property that keeps roster cost linear, not quadratic, in child\n  count.\n- Regression: the existing plan-review unit suite stays green.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `ac-satisfiability` is a registered plan-review criterion routed as `exec: \"1-TURN\"`,\n      `facet: \"coherence\"`, `default_posture: \"advisory\"`; verified by\n      `pytest tests/unit/test_ac_satisfiability_criterion.py::test_routing_entry` and by\n      `validate_packaged_routing()` returning an empty list.\n- [ ] Its rubric states the set-quantification primitive and names all three contradiction\n      shapes (universal-vs-carve-out, derived-artifact closure, snapshot cardinality), and does\n      not reference the `Success Criteria` migration that motivated it; verified by\n      `pytest tests/unit/test_ac_satisfiability_criterion.py::test_rubric_is_generic`.\n- [ ] `rebar explain ac-satisfiability` prints the criterion's guide section; verified by\n      running that command and observing a non-empty section.\n- [ ] Nothing under `src/rebar/llm/review_kernel/` changes, because Pass-2's\n      `internal_conflict` axis already grades this defect; verified by\n      `git diff --exit-code origin/main -- src/rebar/llm/review_kernel/` exiting 0.\n- [ ] The sibling roster fed to the container pass carries each child's acceptance-criteria\n      items, not only ids and titles; verified by\n      `pytest tests/unit/test_container_roster.py::test_roster_carries_child_ac`.\n- [ ] A child description with no `## Acceptance Criteria` section, and a parent with no\n      children, each produce a roster without raising; verified by\n      `pytest tests/unit/test_container_roster.py::test_roster_degrades_gracefully`.\n- [ ] `G3`'s rubric instructs the reviewer to test \"no sibling covers it\" against the roster's\n      acceptance criteria; verified by\n      `pytest tests/unit/test_container_roster.py::test_g3_rubric_cites_roster_ac`.\n- [ ] The container pass sends the roster inside its system prompt and not inside its\n      per-pairing instructions; verified by\n      `pytest tests/unit/test_container_roster.py::test_roster_rides_cached_prefix`.\n- [ ] `G3`'s `default_posture` and `block_threshold` are unchanged from their pre-change values;\n      verified by `pytest tests/unit/test_container_roster.py::test_g3_posture_unchanged`.\n- [ ] `docs/plan-review-criteria-guide.md` parity-checks clean against the registry; verified by\n      `validate_criteria_guide()` returning an empty list.\n- [ ] No `src/rebar/**/*.py` file exceeds the 800-LOC cap; verified by\n      `pytest tests/unit/test_module_size_contract.py`.\n- [ ] `make lint`, `make typecheck` and `pytest tests/unit` all pass.","id":"0d87-6e23-a894-4d0a","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":["plan-review","gate","criteria"],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Plan review: own AC joint-satisfiability, and let G3's child-coverage audit fire"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785375955713909001,"uuid":"81e69b81-563f-4add-99a2-c12d5f7dc717"}